BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53188719243390
DTSTART:20260903T220000Z
DTEND:20260903T234500Z
DESCRIPTION:This year\, NC State LIVE and the Gregg Museum of Art & Design 
 are joining forces to present concerts focused on community connection and
  well-being.\n\nJohn “Lakota John” Locklear is a living representation
  of the musical cultures of southeastern North Carolina. He is a member of
  the Lumbee Tribe that combines the blues with the indigenous music of his
  ancestors. Locklear first picked up the harmonica at age 7\, then the gui
 tar. At age 10\, intrigued by the sound of the slide guitar\, he bought a 
 glass slide\, placed it on his pinky finger and has been sliding ever sinc
 e. Mama Tonya\, Papa John and Sister Layla join him to form a proud Lumbee
  Nation family of talented musicians.\n\nIn connection with this event\, p
 lease visit these Gregg Museum exhibitions: Stories Told by Breath: Native
  American Voices in North Carolina and A Creek in Carolina: Artworks by Bo
 bby C. Martin. They will be on display through Sept. 26.\n\nThis event is 
 free\, but registration is required.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design\, Front Lawn 
SUMMARY:NC State LIVE Presents Lakota John at the Gregg Museum
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/nc-state-live-presents-lakota
 -john-on-the-lawn-at-the-gregg-museum-of-art-design
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53753853385806
DTSTART:20260916T223000Z
DTEND:20260917T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh’s Poet Laureate Johnny Lee Chapman III will organize 
 and facilitate a series of intergenerational Story Circles at the Gregg Mu
 seum of Art & Design. Centered on the voices of Black elders in Raleigh\, 
 these gatherings are designed to connect deeply with young adults — the 
 next generation of leaders. The Gregg is hosting the series as part of its
  ongoing exploration of sanctuary\, community memory and collective care.\
 n\nThe Story Circles are inspired by two exhibitions at the museum: Crafti
 ng Sanctuaries: Black Spaces of the Great Depression South and Community S
 tories: Sustaining North Carolina Black Heritage.\n\nThrough facilitated d
 ialogue and storytelling\, the series will explore how Black communities i
 n Raleigh built networks of mutual support and sanctuary. These gatherings
  will create powerful opportunities for Black elders to pass down lived hi
 stories and welcome all NC State students and the broader community to joi
 n in intergenerational dialogue and preserving vital community memory.\n\n
 The featured guest speaker for this Story Circles event will be Octavia Ra
 iney\, a lifelong advocate for justice whose leadership has shaped communi
 ties across Raleigh and beyond for over 50 years.\n\nSpace is limited to p
 reserve the intimacy of the circle. Registration is required for this free
  event.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Intergenerational Story Circle Series With Johnny Lee Chapman III
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/intergenerational-story-circl
 e-series-with-johnny-lee-chapman-iii
CATEGORIES:Lectures and Talks
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53188747520182
DTSTART:20261001T190000Z
DTEND:20261001T203000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join the Gregg Museum of Art & Design for a compelling afternoo
 n of art\, history and community reflection as we explore the history and 
 cultural significance of North Carolina Freedom Park. Led by Freedom Park 
 Manager Khadija McNair\, this exclusive program will offer an insider's lo
 ok at how art and public spaces intersect to honor the enduring journey to
 ward freedom. McNair\, who earned a Bachelor of Arts and a Master of Arts 
 in history at North Carolina Central University\, is driven by her passion
  to share the stories of those who have shaped the places we call home.  \
 n\nA Q&A session will follow McNair's presentation. The program will concl
 ude with a curated walk-through of the exhibition\, Community Stories: Sus
 taining North Carolina Black Heritage. \n\nThis event is free\, but regist
 ration is required.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Exploring Liberty\, Resilience and Inspiration With Khadija McNair
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/talk-and-tour-with-khadija-mc
 nair-nc-freedom-park-manager-exploring-liberty-resilience-and-inspiration
CATEGORIES:Lectures and Talks
CATEGORIES:Student Life
CATEGORIES:Information Session
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54000626693299
DTSTART:20261005T200000Z
DTEND:20261005T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Visit the Gregg Museum of Art & Design for the opening of the e
 xhibition Remembering & Resisting: LGBTQ+ History From Nazi Germany to Tod
 ay and a keynote address by curator Jake Newsome\, founder of The Pink Tri
 angle Legacies Project. Newsome's keynote address\, "Enemies of the State:
  The Nazi Persecution of LGBTQ+ People\," will examine the complex motivat
 ions behind Nazi leaders' choice to persecute LGBTQ+ people and to present
  it as vital to the success of the Third Reich. A Q&A and a book signing w
 ill follow the presentation. Please register to attend.\n\nRemembering & R
 esisting: LGBTQ+ History From Nazi Germany to Today is a traveling exhibit
  that explores the persecution of queer and trans people under Nazi rule a
 nd the long\, ongoing struggle to reclaim their histories. Beginning in We
 imar Germany’s early LGBTQ+ movements\, the exhibit traces how the Nazi 
 regime targeted those deemed “undesirable” and how survivors faced con
 tinued criminalization and silence long after 1945. The exhibition will be
  on display at the Gregg Museum through Oct. 23.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design\, Main Lobby
SUMMARY:Remembering & Resisting: LGBTQ+ History From Nazi Germany to Today
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/exhibition-opening-and-keynot
 e-address-dr-jake-newsome-the-pink-triangle-legacies-project-enemies-of-th
 e-state-the-nazi-persecution-of-lgbtq-people
CATEGORIES:Community Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53188773917017
DTSTART:20261007T211500Z
DTEND:20261007T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:The Terrain Dance Project is pleased to present "Embodied Futur
 es: Dance\, Plant Science and Public Engagement" at NC State’s Gregg Mus
 eum of Art & Design.\nThe company will present works made possible by an A
 rts + Science mini-grant from the Office of University Interdisciplinary P
 rograms and Arts NC State\, exploring human and plant relationships\, ethn
 obotany\, crop genetics\, heirloom corn\, crop processing and NC sweetpota
 toes!\n\nIn collaboration with Arts NC State\, the evening will include a 
 showcase of work by various Arts + Science mini-grant recipients\, leading
  up to a performance in the museum’s garden\, a film screening and a pan
 el discussion. This event will showcase and celebrate the innovative resea
 rch and interdisciplinary work at NC State and beyond through art\, perfor
 mance and conversation.\n\nPlease RSVP. Rain date: October 14\, same time 
 and location.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Embodied Futures: Dance\, Plant Science and Public Engagement
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/embodied-futures-dance-plant-
 science-and-public-engagement-presented-by-terrain-dance-project-at-the-gr
 egg-museum-of-art-design
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54099767102537
DTSTART:20261029T190000Z
DTEND:20261029T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join artist Maya Freelon for an immersive gallery tour of Groun
 ded\, exploring the evolution of her work\, creative practice\, and the Fr
 eelon lineage and legacy. Through conversation\, "slow looking"\, gentle m
 ovement\, awareness\, and discovery\, participants will engage with the te
 xtures\, colors\, materials\, and memories embedded in Freelon’s work.\n
 \nThe tour will include artwork by Maya Freelon\, alongside photographs by
  her late father\, Phil Freelon — NC State Design School alum and archit
 ect of the renovated Gregg Museum of Art & Design — and her great-grandf
 ather\, Allan Freelon\, Sr.\, a pioneer African American Impressionist pai
 nter active during the Harlem Renaissance.\n\nThe exhibition space welcome
 s the practice of grounding\, as Maya guides visitors through mindful mome
 nts that evoke the senses and invite reflection on ancestry\, healing\, jo
 y\, resilience\, and connection. Participants will leave with grounding pr
 actices they can carry beyond the museum\, using mindfulness as a tool for
  Grounding wherever they go.\n\nThis event is free but registration is req
 uired.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Guided Tour and Meditation with Maya Freelon
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/guided-tour-and-meditation-wi
 th-maya-freelon
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Training
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
CATEGORIES:Student Life
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261003T050349Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54100007281045
DTSTART:20261107T180000Z
DTEND:20261107T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a fun-filled afternoon at the Gregg Museum of Art &
  Design to celebrate the opening of Grounded by acclaimed artist Maya Free
 lon. \n\n \n\nDrawing on her signature use of delicate tissue paper\, Free
 lon transforms the galleries into an immersive environment that honors bot
 h fragility and resilience. In Grounded\, the red clay and soil of North C
 arolina become central materials and metaphors\, asking us to consider wha
 t it means to create sanctuary in uncertain times and how the land itself 
 holds memory\, pain\, and possibility.\n\n \n\nThe community celebration w
 ill include local organizations highlighting African American heritage and
  wellness\, along with special performances by Tre. Charles\, Johnny Lee C
 hapman III\, and NC State’s Panoramic Dance Project. \n\n \n\nYou are in
 vited to Connect. Celebrate. Get Grounded. Please read more about the time
 d activities below.\n\nCelebration Program Schedule\n\n1 p.m. | Celebratio
 n Begins\n\nEnjoy ongoing activities throughout the museum and grounds fro
 m 1-5 p.m.\, including a collaborative Tissue Paper Quilting activity insi
 de the Grounded exhibition\, community organization tables\, a food truck 
 and more. Complimentary non-alcoholic beverages and light treats will be p
 rovided. \n\n1:15–1:45 p.m. | Panoramic Dance\nExperience a special perf
 ormance choreographed by Christa Oliver and featuring NC State students. T
 he performance begins outside the museum’s main entrance\, inviting visi
 tors to follow the dancers into the galleries for a brief performance befo
 re moving together outdoors. Accessible seating will be available for visi
 tors who need accommodations. The program is primarily designed for standi
 ng-room viewing. \n\n1:45–2:10 p.m. | Welcome and Opening Remarks with a
 rtist Maya Freelon\nOpening remarks with artist Maya Freelon\, Gregg Museu
 m Director Sara Segerlin\, and special guests\, at the concert stage by th
 e front lawn.\n\n2:15–3:45 p.m. | Tre. Charles concert at the Museum Law
 n\nEnjoy an outdoor concert on the lawn featuring Tre. Charles and band.  
 Please feel free to bring a lawn chair or blanket.\n\n2:45–3 p.m. and 3:
 30–3:45 p.m. | Spoken Word with Johnny Lee Chapman\nRaleigh Poet Laureat
 e Johnny Lee Chapman presents two intimate spoken word performances in Cra
 fting Sanctuaries.\n\n3:45–4:15 p.m. | Panoramic Dance\nA second opportu
 nity to experience Panoramic Dance with Christa Oliver and NC State studen
 ts\, beginning outside the museum entrance and moving through the gallerie
 s and outdoors. Accessible seating will be available for visitors who need
  accommodations. The program is primarily designed for standing-room viewi
 ng. \n\n5 p.m. | Celebration Concludes\n\nFree and open to the public. Dro
 p in anytime between 1 and 5 p.m.  \n\nParking: The Gregg Museum parking l
 ot will be closed for the celebration. Visitors may park in the Ferndell p
 ublic parking lot or at Pullen Arts Center\, where parking is free on Satu
 rdays. These alternate parking locations are approximately 2–4 blocks fr
 om the museum. Please visit the Gregg Museum website for additional parkin
 g information: https://gregg.arts.ncsu.edu/about/visit/alternate-parking-l
 ocations-for-the-gregg-museum/
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Opening Celebration of Grounded by Maya Freelon
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/opening-celebration-of-ground
 ed-by-maya-freelon
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
